meta {
  name: Submit Public Lead
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/public/leads/{{shareLinkToken}}
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "primaryContact": {
      "firstName": "Robin",
      "lastName": "Nakamura",
      "dateOfBirth": "1985-03-21",
      "phone": "(555) 777-8888",
      "email": "robin.nakamura@example.com"
    },
    "address": {
      "street": "5 Bridge Street",
      "city": "Tulsa",
      "state": "OK",
      "zip": "74110"
    },
    "members": [],
    "submissionToken": "{{$guid}}"
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 201 Created without any Authorization header", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("returns a plain confirmation — no ids, no record details", function () {
    expect(res.getBody()).to.eql({ submitted: true });
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /public/leads/:token — submit a lead through a share link (PAC-37)

  **Public** (`@Public()`): no JWT, no `AccessContext`. Runs the *identical*
  intake pipeline as `POST /leads`, so a returning client resolves onto their
  existing contact and household instead of creating duplicates.

  Run **Create Share Link** first — it captures `shareLinkToken`.

  ## Body
  Same shape as `POST /leads`, **minus `leadSourceCode`**. Lead source is
  internal vocabulary (Quotewizard, Soleo, Data Lot, JYA) and is never shown to
  an outside submitter.

  ## Tenancy comes only from the link
  `agencyId`, `branchId` and `producerId` are read from the stored share-link
  record. Injecting them into the body does nothing — twice over: the zod schema
  strips unknown keys, and `LeadIntakeService` takes tenancy exclusively from an
  `IntakeContext` built by the caller, never from the payload. Two independent
  layers, so neither alone is load-bearing.

  ## Lead source is left unset
  The created lead stores `{ "code": null, "label": "" }`, which the list
  endpoint normalizes to the label **"Unknown"**, and a producer sets the real
  source by hand later. Guessing a default would record an
  assumption as fact, and once written nothing could distinguish it from a
  genuine answer. Producers find these with `GET /leads?leadSource=__none__`.

  ## Provenance
  Every lead records `intakeSource.channel` (`share_link`) and
  `intakeSource.shareLinkId`, so a producer can tell an externally-submitted
  record from one they typed in, and a misbehaving link is traceable to the
  records it produced before being revoked.

  > **Accepted risk:** someone who knows a real client's name and date of birth
  > can, through a public link, write into that client's household. This was a
  > deliberate call — duplicate-free matching was judged more valuable than
  > closing that vector. Provenance stamping is the mitigation.

  ## Rate limiting
  `5 / minute` **and** `30 / hour` per IP by default
  (`PUBLIC_INTAKE_RATE_LIMIT`, `PUBLIC_INTAKE_HOURLY_LIMIT`). Two windows: the
  per-minute limit stops a burst, the hourly one catches a slow drip that would
  sit under it all day. The throttler guard runs *first* in the global chain, so
  a flood is rejected before any database work.

  ## Response `201`
  ```json
  { "submitted": true }
  ```
  No lead id, no household id, no record details — the submitter is an outsider.

  `submissionCount` on the link increments for **new** leads only, so a retry
  over a flaky mobile connection doesn't inflate it.

  ## Errors
  - `400 Bad Request` — validation failure.
  - `404 Not Found` — the same generic "This form is no longer available." as
    the GET, for an unknown/revoked/malformed token, an inactive agency, a
    disabled module, or a deactivated producer. Nothing is written.
  - `429 Too Many Requests` — rate limit exceeded; rejected before the pipeline
    runs, so nothing is written.
}
